      "chapterNumber": 1,
      "headline": "Being Wrong Less",
      "keyInsight": "We love being right, but our brains constantly play tricks to keep us comfortable. Most people defend their existing beliefs like a fortress under siege. When new facts arrive, they treat them like enemy invaders instead of helpful visitors. This is why smart adults stay stuck in dead-end routines or bad investments. True mental strength does not mean knowing every answer upfront. It means spotting your own blind spots before life forces you to. Think of opinions as disposable sticky notes, not permanent granite statues. When you treat mistakes as useful compass readings, being wrong suddenly feels like uncovering treasure. The fastest way to win is wanting the truth more than wanting to look clever.",
      "content": "Imagine baking a fancy chocolate cake from memory. You mix flour, sugar, and cocoa, but the cake comes out flat and bitter. A stubborn baker blames the oven, the weather, or bad cocoa beans. A wise baker checks the counter and realizes they grabbed salt instead of sugar. Life works the exact same way. Most of us run around defending bad cakes because admitting an error hurts our pride. The secret to smarter decisions is simple: stop trying to be right, and start trying to be wrong less often.\n\nThink about headlights on a dark country road. When you drive at night, your brights reveal sharp curves and fallen branches early. If you only look at your rear-view mirror, you will crash into the nearest ditch. Yet in daily life, people stare into the rear-view mirror constantly. They collect evidence to prove past choices were wise, ignoring the massive tree right in front of them. When you actively look for counter-arguments, you switch on high-beam headlights for your mind. You spot bad turns before your wheels leave the pavement.\n\nTake the classic habit of stripping a problem down to its bolts. Think of a master bicycle mechanic fixing a strange rattling noise. Instead of buying a whole new bike, the mechanic takes off the chain, wipes the gears, and checks each spoke. They find one loose nut, tighten it, and the ride turns silky smooth again. When you strip away assumptions and examine the basic parts, big scary problems shrink into small, manageable repairs.",
