      "id": "super-thinking-the-big-book-of-mental-models-ch-6",
      "order": 6,
      "chapterNumber": 6,
      "headline": "Decisions, Decisions",
      "keyInsight": "Whenever we face a tough fork in the road, our brains crave complete certainty. We stall, gather more pamphlets, check five more reviews, and let golden hours slip down the drain. But waiting is itself a weighty choice with its own steep bill. Every hour spent agonizing over the perfect choice steals focus from the project already sitting on your desk. Furthermore, we cling desperately to past investments. You finish a bland, salty bowl of soup just because you already handed the cashier ten dollars. You stay inside a drafty theater watching a boring movie just because the ticket cost money. That cash is gone forever whether you smile or suffer. Smart decision makers look through the windshield, not the rearview mirror. They balance the odds, make the call, and leave yesterday's bill in the wastebasket.",
      "content": "Imagine standing in a packed airport luggage store. You have two minutes before boarding your flight. You spot a sturdy canvas duffel bag that checks every box. It is spacious, weatherproof, and reasonably priced. But you hesitate. What if the boutique three terminals away carries an even sleeker shade of navy blue? You sprint across the terminal, miss your boarding call, and get stranded overnight. Hunting for the mythical best option often destroys a perfectly great outcome right in front of you.\n\nEvery choice carries an invisible price tag. Economists call this the trade-off cost. When you spend Saturday morning repainting the hallway wall, the true cost is not just thirty dollars of latex paint. The real price is the brisk mountain hike you skipped and the quiet book chapter you never read. Life does not give you refund vouchers for misspent hours. Whenever you say yes to one path, you are quietly whispering no to twenty alternative adventures.\n\nTo make cleaner choices, divide your doors into two categories. Some doors swing both ways. If you paint your front door olive green and hate it next week, you can repaint it on Sunday. Walk through those doors quickly without agonizing. Other doors lock behind you forever, like selling a family homestead. Approach those one-way doors with a slow lantern and a steady map. Treat decisions like a craft, not a roll of the dice.",
      "keyTakeaway": "Past costs belong to history; only future possibilities deserve your attention.",
      "audioSnippetText": "Indecision is often the most expensive choice you can make. Cut past losses quickly and focus entirely on the open road ahead.",
