      "chapterNumber": 7,
      "headline": "Cooling Down Fights Before They Boil Over",
      "keyInsight": "When arguments blow up, we usually assume the other person is evil or foolish. In reality, bad systems and bad incentives create bad behavior. Think of two drivers fighting over one parking spot. Neither person woke up wanting to ruin your morning. Both are reacting to crowded streets, poor signs, and ticking clocks. If you treat conflict like an open flame, pouring emotional fuel makes it roar. Instead, step back and look at the hidden rules causing the collision. When you separate the person from the messy situation, solutions appear almost like magic. Most fights are not about bad apples. They are about broken barrels.",
      "content": "Picture two chefs bumping elbows in a tiny, chaotic restaurant kitchen. Orders are piling up fast. One chef shouts because the knives are dull, while the other yells because the stove is blocked. It feels personal, but it is really a design problem. The room has poor flow and nobody assigned clear stations. In everyday life, most conflict stems from mismatched expectations rather than bad intentions.\n\nTake the classic dinner dispute over who washes the dishes. If nobody sets an agreement ahead of time, each person feels like they carry the whole burden. One person feels unappreciated, while the other feels unfairly attacked. A tiny conversation before cooking begins solves the whole drama instantly. Clear ground rules remove friction before sparks can catch fire.\n\nAnother trap is the blame game. When a project slips off schedule, pointing fingers just makes everyone defensive. Instead of asking who broke the clock, look at how the gears were set up. Were deadlines realistic? Did everyone have the right tools? When you fix the underlying setup, people work together smoothly, like a well-tuned bicycle cruising down a paved path.",
